      "published_at": "2026-06-09T23:55:34Z",
      "body": "Version 1.8.11 - Fix Hearing Impaired false positives on title \"Hi\"\n\nThe auto-detection regex `\\bhi\\b` and `.hi.` were matching any \"Hi\" in a release\nfilename, falsely flagging ~500 episodes and ~100 movies as Hearing Impaired.\n\nForum-reported failing cases (now correctly classified as NOT HI):\n  - The.Hi-Lo.Country.1998.1080p.USA.BluRay.AVC.DTS-HD.MA.5.1-rmHD\n  - The.King.of.Queens.S03E13.Hi.Def.Jam.1080p.NF.WEB-DL.AAC2.0.H.264-POWER\n  - For.All.Mankind.S01E07.Hi.Bob.1080p.WEB-DL.6CH.x265.HEVC-PSA\n\nFix in src/services/subtitleUploadService.js:\n  - checkHearingImpairedFromString: dropped bare \\bhi\\b / hi[_-] / [_-]hi.\n    Now only fires on sdh, psdh, hearing.impaired variants, or a path part\n    that IS exactly \"hi\" (e.g. an /HI/ directory).\n  - checkHearingImpairedFromFilename: switched to positional token matching.\n    Strip subtitle extension, tokenize on .-_, then look for hi/sdh/psdh/\n    hearingimpaired only in the LAST 3 TOKENS — the language-code / feature-\n    flag slot where real HI markers live (movie.eng.hi.srt, show.sdh.srt).\n    hearing.impaired keyword still matches anywhere.\n\nReal HI markers in the language-code slot still detect correctly:\n  - movie.hi.srt, movie.eng.hi.srt, movie-hi-en.srt, show.s01e01.sdh.srt\n\nTests: 48 pass (38 pre-existing + 10 new regression tests covering the\nthree forum cases and reverse-direction \"must still detect\" sanity checks).",
